Boils (Furuncles/Carbuncles)
Dermatology & Soft Tissue
Painful firm inflammatory nodule, Central suppuration/pointing, Fluctuant mass

VISIT CONTEXT

Case ID: DST-009
Reason for Visit: Rapidly expanding, exquisitely painful, fluctuant mass on the posterior neck associated with systemic malaise.
Location: Private residence
Present at Visit: Patient only
Urgency Justification: Telephone triage reported a rapidly enlarging, highly painful mass on the nape of the neck with associated low-grade fever and systemic malaise. Urgent assessment was required to evaluate the extent of the abscess, determine the immediate need for incision and drainage, and initiate systemic antibiotics to prevent widespread cellulitis or sepsis prior to the next in-hours period.

SUBJECTIVE

Chief Complaint: "There is a massive, throbbing lump on the back of my neck. I can't move my head, and I've been getting chills all afternoon."
History of Present Illness: The patient noted a small, tender red bump on the nape of the neck 4 days ago. Over the past 48 hours, there has been rapid enlargement into an extremely painful, firm, erythematous inflammatory nodule. Yesterday, the patient noted central suppuration presenting as a yellow-white pustular tip. Today, the pain has become throbbing and continuous, accompanied by a low-grade fever, systemic malaise, and stiffness in the neck due to the mechanical bulk of the lesion. The development of a fluctuant, tender mass indicates mature abscess formation.
Functional Status: Independent, but neck mobility is severely restricted by pain.
Social/Safety Concerns: Lives alone; risk of systemic sepsis if left untreated.

OBJECTIVE

Vitals: BP: 130/80 | HR: 95 | Temp: 38.2 | RR: 16 | SpO2: 99% | Wt: 88 Kg
General: Uncomfortable, guarding the neck, holding head in a rigid forward posture.
Physical Exam: On the nape of the neck, there is a 5x5 cm firm, highly erythematous, and exquisitely tender inflammatory nodule. In the center of the indurated plaque, there is a 2 cm area of pronounced fluctuance with a distinct yellow-white pustular tip (pointing) indicating impending spontaneous rupture. The surrounding skin is hot and erythematous, extending 2 cm beyond the induration. Palpation of the posterior cervical chain reveals tender regional lymphadenopathy. No visible draining sinus tracts or aggregate carbuncle formation yet.
Environmental Observations: Clean environment.
Medication Reconciliation: Checked against EHR. No current antibiotics. No history of MRSA.

ASSESSMENT

Primary Diagnosis: Large Cervical Furuncle (Abscess) with localized cellulitis - Status: Acute Exacerbation
Differentials:

Epidermoid Cyst (Ruptured/Infected) - Ruled out/unlikely due to: The absence of a pre-existing long-standing painless lump and lack of visible punctum; presentation is typical for a primary staphylococcal follicular infection.

Carbuncle - Ruled out/unlikely due to: The lesion remains a single contiguous fluctuant mass without multiple distinct draining sinus tracts, though it is large enough to border on this classification.

Deep Neck Space Infection - Ruled out/unlikely due to: Infection is clearly localized to the dermal and subcutaneous tissue with visible superficial pointing, lacking signs of airway compromise or deep fascial involvement.
Risk Level: Environmental/Social Risk: Low

CLINICAL REASONING SUMMARY

The patient presents with a large, mature, pointing furuncle complicated by local cellulitis and systemic symptoms (fever, malaise). Triage accurately recognized the systemic symptoms and rapid expansion as high-risk for impending sepsis. Urgent after-hours assessment was clinically mandated to evaluate the mass for immediate bedside surgical drainage versus definitive hospital transfer, and to immediately initiate targeted oral antibiotics. Delaying this review would likely have resulted in spontaneous, uncontrolled rupture, expanding cellulitis, or worsening systemic toxicity requiring emergency admission.

CLINICIAN CARE PLAN

Clinical Interventions: The lesion is pointing and highly fluctuant. After informed consent and local anesthesia (Lignocaine 1% with adrenaline), a simple bedside incision and drainage was performed, releasing 15ml of thick, purulent material. The cavity was flushed with saline and a sterile absorbent dressing applied. Commenced oral Flucloxacillin 500mg QID for 7 days for the surrounding cellulitis.
Positive Behavioral Change: By taking your antibiotics exactly four times a day on an empty stomach, you will ensure the highest amount of medicine reaches the skin, rapidly killing the remaining bacteria and stopping the redness from spreading. By applying a fresh, warm compress over the dressing four times a day, you will increase local blood flow, which will help your body flush out the remaining infection and dramatically reduce the swelling.
Home-Based Services (Optional): Not required.
DME/Safety (Optional): Not required.
Follow-up: GP review in 48 hours for a wound check, dressing change, and evaluation of the cellulitis resolution.

SAFETY-NETTING ESCALATION PLAN

If the red area continues to spread down your back, if you develop a high fever over 39.0C, confusion, or if the pain suddenly worsens despite the drainage, present immediately to the Emergency Department.

PATIENT INFORMATION

Disclaimer: Wound infections can progress even with antibiotics. The drained cavity must heal from the bottom up.
Clinical Course: The throbbing pain should improve almost immediately following the drainage. The surrounding redness and fever should subside over the next 48 hours on antibiotics.
SAFETY-NETTING ADVICE

Seek emergency care if redness spreads rapidly, you become dizzy, or if you cannot tolerate fluids.
PATIENT EDUCATION

Treatment Explanation: The lump was a pocket of pus that your body could not clear alone. Opening it relieved the pressure, and the antibiotics will clear the bacteria that started the infection in the hair follicle.
Actionable Steps: Keep the dressing clean and dry. Wash your hands thoroughly before and after touching the neck area to prevent spreading the bacteria to other parts of your body.

